#!/bin/sh
# Switch the active orchard-themes theme. Repoints the
# ~/.config/orchard-themes/active symlink to the named theme dir
# and signals every app that supports runtime reload to pick up the
# change. Open ghostty windows update in place.
#
# Usage:
#   set-theme                  list available themes + show current
#   set-theme <name>           switch to <name>
#
# Adding a new app to the theme system: drop a partial named
# <app>.<ext> under each theme dir, point the app's main config at
# ~/.config/orchard-themes/active/<app>.<ext>, and add the app's
# reload command at the bottom of this script (or no-op if the app
# auto-watches its config file).
set -eu

themes_dir=$HOME/.config/orchard-themes
target=${1:-}

if [ -z "$target" ]; then
    echo "Available themes:"
    for d in "$themes_dir"/*/; do
        [ -d "$d" ] || continue
        name=$(basename "$d")
        echo "  $name"
    done
    echo
    if [ -L "$themes_dir/active" ]; then
        echo "Current: $(readlink "$themes_dir/active")"
    else
        echo "Current: (no active theme set)"
    fi
    exit 0
fi

if [ ! -d "$themes_dir/$target" ]; then
    echo "set-theme: '$target' not found under $themes_dir" >&2
    echo "Available:" >&2
    for d in "$themes_dir"/*/; do
        [ -d "$d" ] || continue
        echo "  $(basename "$d")" >&2
    done
    exit 1
fi

# Atomic-ish swap. `ln -sfn` replaces the symlink in place; the
# rename window is small enough that no consumer of the path sees a
# half-state.
ln -sfn "$target" "$themes_dir/active"

# Kick off the slow nvim updates first, in parallel, so they run
# concurrently with everything else below. nvim is the slowest
# consumer (subprocess spawn per socket + colorscheme load inside
# the running instance), so left at the end of the script it was
# always visibly last to repaint. Each `nvim --server ... --remote-
# expr ...` is backgrounded with `&` so multiple running nvims
# update simultaneously rather than serially. We don't `wait` —
# the rest of the script continues immediately, and the nvim
# instances finish their reloads on their own.
#
# We use --remote-expr (not --remote-send) for two reasons:
#   1. --remote-send interprets the input through nvim's keymap
#      system, so any <C-\> we'd send as a mode-exit gets eaten by
#      vim-tmux-navigator and bounces our tmux focus to the
#      previous pane.
#   2. --remote-expr is a direct expression eval — no keystrokes,
#      no mode juggling. We just call execute() with the colon
#      command.
#
# stdio is redirected to /dev/null so the briefly-spawned nvim
# client can't query terminal capabilities (would leak
# ^[[?997;1n into the shell's input buffer) or echo the empty
# execute() result.
runtime_dir=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}
for sock in "$runtime_dir"/nvim.*.0; do
    [ -S "$sock" ] || continue
    nvim --server "$sock" --remote-expr \
        "execute('luafile ~/.config/orchard-themes/active/nvim.lua')" \
        </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
done

# Wallpaper next, because it's the show: set-wallpaper sweeps the new image
# out of the center of the screen over half a second (awww's `center`
# transition), and every reload below lands inside that window, so the whole
# theme switch reads as one motion instead of a dozen repaints. The theme
# owns a wallpaper, so the switch may have changed which image ought to be
# up — say so, and let set-wallpaper work it out. Note that won't
# necessarily be *this* theme's wallpaper: an image chosen with
# set-wallpaper deliberately outranks the theme and survives theme
# switches, so trying a wallpaper on isn't undone by the next set-theme.
"$HOME/.local/bin/set-wallpaper" || true

# Ghostty reloads on SIGUSR2 specifically. Sending any other signal
# crashes it, so the explicit -SIGUSR2 + -x are both load-bearing.
pkill -SIGUSR2 -x ghostty 2>/dev/null || true

# Hyprland window borders pull their color from this theme dir's
# hyprland.conf via the main config's `source = .../active/hyprland.conf`.
# A `hyprctl reload` re-reads the config (following the freshly-repointed
# active symlink) so the focused-window border repaints to the new theme.
# reload doesn't re-run exec-once, so daemons started at launch aren't
# disturbed.
hyprctl reload >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

# eww OSD (the volume/brightness square) — regenerate its themed colours from
# the new palette, then restart eww so it repaints from them. A restart rather
# than `eww reload`: reloading in place can strand a layer surface the daemon
# then loses track of, leaving a ghost square stuck on screen (see restart-osd).
# Skipped cleanly on a machine without the volume-and-brightness-controls
# feature.
if command -v eww >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    "$HOME/.local/bin/render-osd-theme" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    "$HOME/.local/bin/restart-osd" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
fi

# Claude usage dropdown — same deal: regenerate its colours from the palette
# and restart its own eww daemon so it repaints from them. Skipped cleanly on
# a machine without the claude-usage feature.
if [ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/render-claude-usage-theme" ]; then
    "$HOME/.local/bin/render-claude-usage-theme" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    "$HOME/.local/bin/restart-claude-usage" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
fi

# Same for the Apple TV remote card, likewise guarded.
if [ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/render-apple-tv-remote-theme" ]; then
    "$HOME/.local/bin/render-apple-tv-remote-theme" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    "$HOME/.local/bin/restart-apple-tv-remote" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
fi

# Waybar hot-reloads CSS via its `reload_style_on_change` watcher
# (set in config.jsonc). Modules keep state on this path, so the
# weather module doesn't blank to refetch and the bar doesn't
# disappear/reappear like it does on SIGUSR2 (which is a full
# reload).
#
# `touch` doesn't work here: it only updates mtime/atime, which
# generates an IN_ATTRIB inotify event — waybar's GFileMonitor
# only fires on IN_MODIFY (real content change). So we rewrite
# the first byte in place with `dd conv=notrunc` — no actual
# content change, but it's a write() call, which generates the
# IN_MODIFY waybar listens for. On reparse, waybar follows the
# `@import` through the freshly-repointed orchard-themes/active
# symlink and picks up the new theme's colors.
style="$HOME/.config/waybar/style.css"
if [ -f "$style" ]; then
    dd if="$style" of="$style" bs=1 count=1 conv=notrunc status=none 2>/dev/null || true
fi

# Chromium picks up theme via /etc/chromium/policies/managed/
# orchard-theme.json, a symlink (set up by chromium/install.sh)
# pointing at this theme dir's chromium.json. Re-reading policy is
# triggered by --refresh-platform-policy on a no-window chromium
# launch — it pushes the change to existing windows live without
# spawning a new one.
if pgrep -x chromium >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    chromium --refresh-platform-policy --no-startup-window </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
fi

# GTK theme — drives chromium's file-picker (via xdg-desktop-portal-
# gtk) and the GNOME apps (Maps, Weather, Calendar, gitg, Characters).
# gsettings writes to dconf; running GTK apps repaint on the next
# settings-changed signal, no restart needed.
gtk_theme_file="$themes_dir/active/gtk-theme-name"
if [ -f "$gtk_theme_file" ]; then
    gtk_theme=$(head -n1 "$gtk_theme_file")
    gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.interface gtk-theme "$gtk_theme" 2>/dev/null || true
fi

# Color scheme — drives `prefers-color-scheme` for chromium (via
# its BrowserColorScheme=0 ("follow device") policy + the freedesktop portal),
# plus GNOME apps that auto-toggle dark/light, plus Claude Code's
# own theme setting (next block). Single source of truth is each
# theme's nvim.lua line `vim.opt.background = "..."`, so adding a
# new theme doesn't require remembering a separate metadata file.
# Without this sync, switching from a dark theme to a light one
# (or vice versa) leaves websites stuck in the previous mode
# because gsettings hadn't been updated.
nvim_lua="$themes_dir/active/nvim.lua"
bg=""
if [ -f "$nvim_lua" ]; then
    bg=$(grep -oE 'vim\.opt\.background[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*"(dark|light)"' "$nvim_lua" \
        | grep -oE '"(dark|light)"' | tr -d '"')
    case "$bg" in
        dark)  gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.interface color-scheme prefer-dark  2>/dev/null || true ;;
        light) gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.interface color-scheme prefer-light 2>/dev/null || true ;;
    esac
fi

# Tmux: re-source the active theme partial in the running server.
# Status bar / pane borders / window tabs all live in that file —
# a `source-file` re-reads it without disturbing sessions, panes,
# or running commands. `tmux info` is a quick "is the server up?"
# check; without it `source-file` would noisily start a server
# just to source one file when no tmux was actually running.
if tmux info >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    tmux source-file ~/.config/orchard-themes/active/tmux.conf 2>/dev/null || true
fi

notify-send "Theme" "Switched to $target" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
